revision.text:
{{draft}}
The {{ill|Munakata clan|ja|宗像氏}} are a Japanese clan that runs [[Munakata Taisha]]<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Matsunaga |first=Naomichi |title=Kuni no miyatsuko |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=8839 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.ph/08goA |archive-date=2023-10-25 |access-date=2023-10-25 |website=Kokugakuin University Encyclopedia of Shinto |language=en}}</ref>. They had advanced nautical skills.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |url=https://www.okinoshima-heritage.jp/en/know/people.html?path=%2Fen%2Fknow%2Fpeople.ctp |access-date=2023-12-01 |website=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |language=en}}</ref>

Read more on this source.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |url=https://www.okinoshima-heritage.jp/en/know/tradition.html?path=%2Fen%2Fknow%2Ftradition.ctp |access-date=2023-12-01 |website=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |language=en}}</ref>

The clan were originally [[Kuni no miyatsuko]] but after the abolition of the role they took on a priestly role at [[Izumo-taisha]], they share this history with the {{ill|Aso clan|ja|阿蘇氏}} of [[Izumo-taisha|Aso Shrine]], the {{Ill|Owari clan|ja|尾張氏}} of [[Atsuta Shrine]], the [[Izumo clan]] of [[Izumo-taisha]],<ref name=":1" /> and the {{ill|Yamato no Kuni no Miyatsuko|lt=Yamato clan|ja|倭国造|simple}} of [[Ōyamato Shrine]].

[[ja:宗像氏]]

==See Also==

* {{ill|Yamato no Kuni no Miyatsuko|lt=Yamato clan|ja|倭国造|simple}}

