The origins of the clan are unclear. Some sources say they descend from the traditional [[Kuni no miyatsuko]] of the region of a common origin with the {{ill|Izumo clan|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}},<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" /> and others say they are a cadet branch of the [[Imperial House of Japan|Imperial family]].

{{ill|Takiribime|ja|タキリビメ|simple|User:Immanuelle/Takiribime|en:draft|}} is described as marrying [[Ōkuninushi|Ōkuninushi]] which links them in with the {{ill|Izumo clan|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}} in myth.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" />

If they were originally [[Kuni no miyatsuko|Kuni no miyatsuko]] and took on a priestly role at [[Munakata Taisha|Munakata Taisha]], they share this history with the {{ill|Aso clan|ja|阿蘇氏|en}} of [[Aso Shrine|Aso Shrine]], the {{Ill|Owari clan|ja|尾張氏|en}} of {{ill|Atsuta Shrine|en}}, the {{ill|Izumo clan|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}} of [[Izumo-taisha|Izumo-taisha]],<ref name="Matsunaga" /> the {{ill|Amabe clan|lt=Amabe clan|ja|海部氏|en}} of {{ill|Kono Shrine|en}}<ref name="Bunka1">{{cite web|title=海部氏系図|trans-title=Amebe shikeizu|url=https://kunishitei.bunka.go.jp/heritage/detail/201/777|publisher={{ill|Agency for Cultural Affairs|en}}|language=Japanese|accessdate=August 20, 2020}}</ref>, and the {{ill|Yamato no Kuni no Miyatsuko|lt=Yamato clan|ja|倭国造|simple}} of [[Ōyamato Shrine|Ōyamato Shrine]].

The confusion may be due to an early interbreeding of them with the Imperial house.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" />



