{{Infobox Japanese clan|province of origin=[[Chikuzen Province]]|home province=[[Chikuzen Province]]|founder=disputed|current head=|image=File:Honden of Munakata Grand Shrine (Hetsu Shrine).JPG|caption=Munakata Taisha Honden|parent house=[[Kuni no miyatsuko]]|image caption=Munakata Taisha Honden|alt=Munakata Taisha Honden}}The {{ill|Munakata clan|ja|宗像氏|simple|User:Immanuelle/Munakata_clan|ja|宗像氏 }} are a Japanese clan that runs [[Munakata Taisha|Munakata Taisha]]<ref name="Matsunaga">{{Cite web |last=Matsunaga |first=Naomichi |title=Kuni no miyatsuko |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=8839 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.ph/08goA |archive-date=2023-10-25 |access-date=2023-10-25 |website=Kokugakuin University Encyclopedia of Shinto |language=en}}</ref>. They had advanced [[sailing]] skills.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |url=https://www.okinoshima-heritage.jp/en/know/people.html?path=%2Fen%2Fknow%2Fpeople.ctp |access-date=2023-12-01 |website=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |language=en}}</ref> They are said to have the same origins as the {{ill|Izumo clan|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}} but care for the daughters of [[Susanoo|Susanoo]] instead of him.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム">{{Cite web|title=Encyclopedia of Shinto詳細|url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=9149|access-date=2024-03-24|website=國學院大學デジタルミュージアム|language=ja}}</ref> 

They are buried in {{ill|Shimbaru-Nuyama Mounded Tomb Group|ja|新原・奴山古墳群}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |url=https://www.okinoshima-heritage.jp/en/know/tradition.html?path=%2Fen%2Fknow%2Ftradition.ctp |access-date=2023-12-01 |website=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region|url=https://www.okinoshima-heritage.jp/en/know/shimbaru.html?path=%2Fen%2Fknow%2Fshimbaru.ctp|access-date=2024-03-20|website=The Sacred Island of Okinoshima and Associated Sites in the Munakata Region|language=en}}</ref>

The clan had close ties with the [[Imperial House of Japan|Imperial family]], engaging in intermarriages to establish alliances throughout the continent. Despite Japan ceasing its diplomatic missions to China, the Munakata Clan maintained trade with continental partners, asserting control over maritime [[trade]] and rights.<ref name="Nogami">{{Cite web |last=Nogami  |first=Takahiro |title=Munakata Shinkō |url=https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=8876 |url-status=live  |archive-date=2023-10-25 |access-date=2023-10-25 |website=Kokugakuin University Encyclopedia of Shinto |language=en}}</ref>

Now, only shrine priests live on Okinoshima, an island with a large stone altar. From 1954 to 1972, research on the island found ancient ceremonies and objects in rocks, dating from the fourth to the ninth centuries. These discoveries helped understand early Shinto rituals and worship.<ref name="Nogami" />

Archaeologists on the island uncovered over 100,000 artifacts, from Japan and places like [[China|China]], [[Korea]] and [[Iran]]. Many are National Treasures or [[Important Cultural Properties of Japan|Important Cultural Properties]]. There are rules against talking about Okinoshima or taking anything from it.<ref name="Nogami" />

== Origins ==
The origins of the clan are unclear. Some sources say they descend from the traditional [[Kuni no miyatsuko]] of the region of a common origin with the {{ill|Izumo clan|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}},<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" /> and others say they are a cadet branch of the [[Imperial House of Japan|Imperial family]].

{{ill|Takiribime|ja|タキリビメ|simple|User:Immanuelle/Takiribime|en:draft|}} is described as marrying [[Ōkuninushi|Ōkuninushi]] which links them in with the {{ill|Izumo clan|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}} in myth.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" />

If they were originally [[Kuni no miyatsuko|Kuni no miyatsuko]] and took on a priestly role at [[Munakata Taisha|Munakata Taisha]], they share this history with the {{ill|Aso clan|ja|阿蘇氏|en}} of [[Aso Shrine|Aso Shrine]], the {{Ill|Owari clan|ja|尾張氏|en}} of {{ill|Atsuta Shrine|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Atsuta Shrine}}, the {{ill|Izumo clan|en|4=simple|5=User:Immanuelle/Izumo clan}} of [[Izumo-taisha|Izumo-taisha]],<ref name="Matsunaga" /> the {{ill|Amabe clan|lt=Amabe clan|ja|海部氏|en}} of {{ill|Kono Shrine|en}}<ref name="Bunka1">{{cite web|title=海部氏系図|trans-title=Amebe shikeizu|url=https://kunishitei.bunka.go.jp/heritage/detail/201/777|publisher={{ill|Agency for Cultural Affairs|en}}|language=Japanese|accessdate=August 20, 2020}}</ref>, and the {{ill|Yamato no Kuni no Miyatsuko|lt=Yamato clan|ja|倭国造|simple}} of [[Ōyamato Shrine|Ōyamato Shrine]].

The confusion may be due to an early interbreeding of them with the Imperial house.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" />



