The confusion may be due to an early interbreeding of them with the Imperial house.<ref name="國學院大學デジタルミュージアム" />
[[ja:宗像氏]]

==Relationship with Izumo==
The origins of the Munakata-no-Kimi, who was granted the title 朝臣 (one of the eight honorary titles known as "八色の姓" or "Yakusa-no-Kabane"), are not mentioned in the Kojiki or Nihonshoki. The {{Ill|Kujiki|en|4=simple|5=user:immanuelle/Kujiki}} provides the origin, stating that the Munakata-no-Kimi and [[Ōkuninushi]] share a common ancestor, Atada-Katasumi-no-Mikoto. This connection suggests a descent from [[Ōkuninushi]], despite the deity being a god rather than a goddess, indicating possible later interpretations.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|151-153}}

The Munakata clan claimed Izumo lineage, reflected in legends such as the birth of the Three Goddesses of Munakata from the ukei between [[Amaterasu]] and [[Susanoo]], and the marriage of [[Ōkuninushi]] to {{ill|Takiribime|ja|タキリビメ|simple|User:Immanuelle/Takiribime|en:draft||fr}}, who resided in Munakata Okutsu-Miya.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|151-153}}

Similarities between Munakata and Izumo include administrative and religious roles, as seen in the analogy between the administrators of Munakata district and Ou district, and the Kannushi of Munakata and Izumo-no-kuni-no-miyatsuko. The Nihonshoki recounts an episode during [[Emperor Sujin]]'s reign where Izumo-Furune, responsible for a sacred treasure, is involved in a fratricidal conflict, linking [[Tsukushi Province]] (Munakata district in Chikuzen) with [[Izumo Province]].<ref name=":0" />{{rp|151-153}}

[[Munakata Taisha]] is the only shrine in the [[Engishiki]] along the Sea of Japan coast. This area contains archaeological sites with burial mounds similar to those in northern Kyūshū, suggesting a connection or settlement by people from the Munakata region.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|151-153}}

The Sea of Japan route connected Munakata with Izumo and further linked Tsuruga with northern Kyūshū, crucial for imperial power. This route may have involved the Okinaga clan from Ōmi Province, explaining the imperial family's focus on sea traffic and Kyūshū during the 7th century.<ref name=":0" />{{rp|151-153}}

